n | First patient dosed in Phase 1/2 trial of RGX-381 in children | Batten Disease News (https://battendiseasenews.com/news/regenxbio-advancing-2-gene-therapies-pipeline-cln2-disease/)\nciteturn630search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: last month; 24, 2023 ...   *   *   *   * A first patient has been dosed in a Phase 1/2 clinical trial testing the gene therapy RGX-381 — one of two in Regenxbio‘s pipeline for late-infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 2 (CLN2) disease — as a treatment for the most common form of Batten disease, the company announced in a second-quarter corporate update.\n\n# Regenxbio advancing 2 gene therapies in pipeline for Batten\n\nFirst patient dosed in Phase 1/2 trial of RGX-381 in children\n\nWritten by Patricia Inácio, PhD | Aug. 24, 2023\n\n  *   Discuss\n  *   Print\n  *   Preferred Source on Google\n\nImage: An illustration of a DNA strand highlights its double-helix structure.\n\n  *   *   *   * A first patient has been dosed in a Phase 1/2 clinical trial testing the gene therapy RGX-381 — one of two in Regenxbio‘s pipeline for late-infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 2 (CLN2) disease — as a treatment for the most common form of Batten disease, the company announced in a second-quarter corporate update.\n\nThe investigational therapy aims to treat eye problems associated with CLN2 disease. According to Regenxbio, the first data from the trial, involving children ages 1-12, are expected next year.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nREGENXBIO (RGNX) Q1 2023 Earnings Call Transcript & Audio (https://stockanalysis.com/stocks/rgnx/transcripts/56850-q1-2023/)\nciteturn630search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.5 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; We expect to initiate dosing in the first in human study of RGX-381 in the first half of 2023. ... REGENXBIO ended the quarter on March 31st, 2023, with cash equivalents, and marketable securities totaling $474 million, compared to $565 million as of December 31st, 2022.\nWe expect to share additional updates on plans for this program in the second half of 2023. Following these two programs, we are also developing RGX-181 to treat neurodegenerative manifestations and RGX-381 to treat ocular manifestations of CLN2 or Batten disease. We expect to initiate dosing in the first in human study of RGX-381 in the first half of 2023. To conclude, we have made significant progress with data updates and trial progression across all programs in our pipeline as we continue working toward our goal of 5 by 25 . At this time, I'd like to thank the patients, families, clinicians, and patient advocacy representatives who are involved in and support these trials without whom these advances in one-time gene therapy development would simply not be possible. With that, I turn the call over to Vit to review our financial guidance. Vit?\n\nVit Vasista\n\nEVP, CFO, and Treasurer, REGENXBIO\n\nThank you, Dr. Pakola. REGENXBIO ended the quarter on March 31st, 2023, with cash equivalents, and marketable securities totaling $474 million, compared to $565 million as of December 31st, 2022. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSEC Filing - Regenxbio Inc (https://regenxbio.gcs-web.com/node/10806/html)\nciteturn630search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.7 years ago; Crawled: 6 days ago; We plan to enroll additional patients in a Cohort 2 expansion of the Phase I/II trial in the first half of 2022.\n\nRGX‑181 and RGX-381 for the Treatment of CLN2 Disease ... In September 2021, REGENXBIO and AbbVie announced a global strategic partnership to develop and commercialize RGX-314, a potential one-time gene therapy for the treatment of wet AMD, DR and other chronic retinal diseases.\n\n \n\nRGX-381 is our investigational AAV Therapeutic targeting the ocular manifestations of CLN2 disease. RGX-381 is designed to use the NAV AAV9 vector to deliver the TPP1 gene directly to the retina. We believe that one-time administration of RGX-381 could provide a durable source of TPP1 activity in the retina, thereby potentially preventing visual decline. There is currently no available treatment for the ocular manifestations of CLN2 disease. We continue to evaluate RGX-381 in preclinical studies and are working with regulatory agencies on the advancement of our clinical program.\n\n \n\nWe have received orphan drug product designation and rare pediatric disease designation from the FDA, as well as ATMP classification from the EMA for RGX‑181 and RGX-381.\n\nCollaborations, Licensing and Company Formation\n\n \n\nCollaborations, licensing and company formation are a key part of our commitment to enable the ongoing development of gene therapy treatments.\n\n \n\nAbbVie Eye Care Collaboration\n\n \n\nIn September 2021, REGENXBIO and AbbVie announced a global strategic partnership to develop and commercialize RGX-314, a potential one-time gene therapy for the treatment of wet AMD, DR and other chronic retinal diseases.\n\n \n\nUnder the terms of our Collaboration and License Agreement with AbbVie (the AbbVie Collaboration and License Agreement), we received an upfront payment of $370 million. Additionally, we will be eligible to receive up to $1.38 billion in additional development, regulatory and commercial milestone payments.\n\n \n\nThrough December 31, 2022, we will be responsible for development expenses for certain ongoing trials of RGX-314 and will share additional development expenses related to RGX-314 with AbbVie. Beginning on January 1, 2023, AbbVie will be responsible for the majority of all development expenses.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTaysha Gene Therapies Provides Update on Deprioritized Pipeline Programs | Batten Disease Family Association (BDFA) (https://bdfa-uk.org.uk/node/447)\nciteturn630search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; On Thursday, February 15, 2024, important news broke regarding CLN1 clinical research that global Batten disease patient advocacy groups wish to share with the Batten community. A patient with CLN1 disease was treated with the investigational gene therapy candidate TSHA-118 under an individual-patient investigator-initiated Investigational New Drug (IND) at RUSH University Medical Centre in Chicago, Illinois.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nREGENXBIO Announces Updated Strategic Plans and Third Quarter 2023 Financial Results | Regenxbio Inc (https://ir.regenxbio.com/news-releases/news-release-details/regenxbio-announces-updated-strategic-plans-and-third-quarter/)\nciteturn630search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.0 years ago; Crawled: 1.1 years ago;   * In October 2023, REGENXBIO reported interim data from the Phase I/II AFFINITY DUCHENNE^{® }trial, demonstrating that RGX-202 continued to be well tolerated with no drug-related serious adverse events in three patients at dose level 1. ...   * REGENXBIO continues to follow patients in the Phase I/II/III CAMPSIITE^{®} trial, with topline results from pivotal phase expected in the first quarter of 2024.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRegenxbio details upcoming RGX-381 trial for CLN2 vision loss | First-in-human study to test gene therapy in children in UK | Batten Disease News (https://battendiseasenews.com/news/regenxbio-details-first-human-trial-rgx-381-cln2-vision-loss/)\nciteturn630search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.7 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; Written by Steve Bryson, PhD | March 2, 2023 ...   *   *   *   * Regenxbio says it will test RGX-381, its investigational gene therapy for eye problems associated with neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 2 (CLN2) disease, in a first-in-human Phase 1/2 clinical trial that remains on track to begin in the first half of this year.\n-------------------------------------------------